    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p22-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Solve the equation\n$$\\frac{1+r+r^2+r^3+\\cdots}{1+r^2+r^4+\\cdots}=\\frac32.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$r=\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "Both series converge when $|r|<1$. Their first terms are $1$ and their common ratios are $r$ and $r^2$ respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1+r+r^2+r^3+\\cdots}{1+r^2+r^4+\\cdots}&=\\frac{\\frac1{1-r}}{\\frac1{1-r^2}}\\\\\\frac{1+r+r^2+r^3+\\cdots}{1+r^2+r^4+\\cdots}&=\\frac{1-r^2}{1-r}\\\\\\frac{1+r+r^2+r^3+\\cdots}{1+r^2+r^4+\\cdots}&=1+r\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}1+r&=\\frac32\\\\r&=\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\left|\\frac12\\right|<1$, this solution is valid.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why the equation\n$$\\frac{1+r+r^2+r^3+\\cdots}{1+r^2+r^4+\\cdots}=\\frac52$$\nhas no solution.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No solution: the equation requires $r=\\frac32$, but the infinite series converge only when $|r|<1$.",
          "worked": "For $|r|<1$, the ratio of the two sums is $1+r$, as in part (a).\n$$\\begin{align*}1+r&=\\frac52\\\\r&=\\frac32.\\end{align*}$$\nThis value violates $|r|<1$: neither infinite series converges. Therefore the equation has no solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
